WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.280
Pixar's brilliant coming-of-age movie&nbsp;
Turning Red is packed with easter eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.960
and deep-cut references to other Pixar and Disney&nbsp;
classics like Luca, Toy Story, Coco, and Mulan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.160
plus the upcoming Lightyear, among many others.
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.600
video I'm revealing the very best&nbsp;
easter eggs and crucial details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.520
you might have missed in Turning Red.
Some mild spoilers ahead, so take care.
&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.160
The Pizza Planet Truck is one of Pixar's&nbsp;
most popular easter eggs and debuted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.880
the studio's first feature, Toy Story. 
In Turning Red, watch carefully as Red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.040
Panda Meilin charges through the streets of&nbsp;
Toronto on her way to the 4*Town concert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.080
speeding past a parked Pizza Planet Truck&nbsp;
with its distinctive white and red rocket.
&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.440
And there's more easter eggs to&nbsp;
the famous animated food brand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.560
including numerous Pizza Planet boxes scattered&nbsp;
around Tyler's house during his birthday party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.600
and an ad for Pizza Planet at the SkyDome&nbsp;
stadium where the 4*Town concert takes place.
&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.080
The Luxo ball, also makes its&nbsp;
appearance at Tyler's birthday party.
&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.160
You can see the iconic yellow, red and&nbsp;
blue ball floating in his swimming pool.
&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.880
The ball first appeared in the short film&nbsp;
Luxo Jr. and interestingly in Mei's room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.400
there's a desk lamp with a similar design&nbsp;
to the famous lamp from that short.
&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.080
Another recurring Pixar easter egg, A113,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.400
appears on screen for just a split&nbsp;
second during the stadium scene.
&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.440
When Mei's dad uses a chalk machine to draw a&nbsp;
circle around his wife's enormous red panda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.400
look very closely at the chalker because&nbsp;
it's a "Professional Model A113".
&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.640
And there's another A113 on the&nbsp;
4*Town tickets in the credits.
&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.360
The presence of A113 in Pixar films is a&nbsp;
tribute to the CalArts classroom where many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.840
of the studio's animators studied.
When Meilin finds herself suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.000
crushing on a boy at school, as he walks by&nbsp;
you'll notice his T-shirt reads "Escápula",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.120
which is a deep-cut reference to Coco and the&nbsp;
heavy-metal band of the same name who performed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.920
in the Land of the Dead's Battle of Bands.
"Escápula" means shoulder blade in Spanish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.760
making it a perfect name for a&nbsp;
band of dead, bony musicians.
&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:07.280
And speaking of bones, Kevin from Up makes an&nbsp;
appearance as a skeleton in a school classroom.
&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.880
In another scene, you can see some Mexican papel&nbsp;
picado decorations like we also saw in Coco.
&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.760
Another nice detail in this scene is how Mei&nbsp;
raises her paws in the air while standing up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.440
similar to how real-life red pandas&nbsp;
will rise up on their back legs when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.400
they're scared in order to look bigger.
Every Pixar movie includes an easter egg to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.520
the next one on the studio's schedule, and&nbsp;
next up is Lightyear, Buzz's origin story.
&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.400
In Turning Red, Meilin's friend Miriam is an&nbsp;
avid skater and if you look at her skateboard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.840
very closely you can spot a sticker of Sox,&nbsp;
Buzz's robotic cat companion in Lightyear.
&nbsp;

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.880
Hello Buzz.
I am Sox, your personal companion robot.
&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.960
Turning Red tips its hat to Luca,&nbsp;
another coming-of-age Pixar story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.440
when Meilin is fantasising about Devon&nbsp;
and draws him as a merman in her notebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.480
a reference to the dual human and sea monster&nbsp;
nature of characters like Luca and Alberto.
&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.160
Mei also has visions of Devon as&nbsp;
a fish during her nightmare when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.880
she first transforms into a red panda.
In Luca, the Turning Red easter egg was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.760
craftily hidden in Giulia's room via&nbsp;
the record titled "4 Star Villaggi".
&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.600
The Italian word "villaggi" means&nbsp;
"villages", similar to the word "town",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.920
making the vinyl record a reference&nbsp;
to Turning Red's boy band 4*Town.
&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.280
And there's another fishy Pixar easter egg&nbsp;
via the Finding Nemo sticker on a stall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.880
in the school bathroom.
Prior to directing Turning Red,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.680
Domee Shi was best known for her short film Bao,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.920
about a Chinese mother living in Toronto&nbsp;
and a dumpling that comes to life.
&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.480
Given, as Shi says, dumplings are "a huge staple&nbsp;
in Chinese culture", it's fitting to find them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.800
repeatedly in Turning Red,
Are you hungry?
&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.480
And there even sign for a restaurant called Bao.
Oh, and there's also the exact same Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.800
decoration on the wall of Meilin's home as&nbsp;
there is in Shi's Oscar-winning short.
&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.120
When Ming makes MeiMei a bowl of&nbsp;
porridge with eggs and a smiley face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.080
it's a little tribute to the happy porridge Mushu&nbsp;
makes for Mulan in the Disney animated movie.
&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.680
Look, you get porridge,&nbsp;
and it's happy to see you.
&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.520
And actor James Hong who plays Mr Gao&nbsp;
in this movie was also the voice of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:15.840
Chi Fu in Disney's much-loved 90s film.
The number of the Lee family home is 234,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.480
a likely reference to the address of Andy's&nbsp;
second house, as revealed in Toy Story 3.
&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.000
Where's home?
Elm Street.
&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.440
234 Elm.
There's an easter egg to the Pixar short film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.440
Purl in MeiMei's bedroom via the little pink ball&nbsp;
of yarn with eyes hanging just above her desk.
&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:36.320
Similar to Turning Red, the theme of Purl is&nbsp;
about learning to accept who you really are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.040
rather than what others want you to be.
The rabbit sticker on Meilin's notebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.360
is the bunny from Burrow, the Pixar short&nbsp;
about a young rabbit who wants to dig the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.840
perfect burrow but ends up embarrassed as she&nbsp;
keeps digging into her neighbours' homes.
&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.160
When we first meet Priya, she's&nbsp;
reading a romantic fantasy novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.800
called "Nightfall: The Final Chapter".
The book's cover, complete with a vampire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.480
feels like a homage to the Twilight&nbsp;
Saga with the central couple giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:09.040
off strong Bella Swan and Edward Cullen vibes.
Fun fact, director Shi says Priya and her deadpan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.680
goth-like manner represents how she and her&nbsp;
friends were into "vampires, punk rock and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.800
emo" around the end of middle school.
Turning Red continues the trend of Luca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.800
with a striking and sometimes&nbsp;
exaggerated animation style, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.560
Shi "infused the film with details from anime".
So, whenever a character gets especially excited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:34.400
their eyes become "super-dilated with sparkles&nbsp;
and stars", a very popular effect in anime.
&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.760
All of which is intended to communicate and signal&nbsp;
the emotional roller coaster of adolescence.
&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:46.400
This shot and pose of Mei leaping across the sky&nbsp;
feels like a specific shout-out to the Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.520
animation "The Girl Who Leapt Through Time".
I love the style of this shot with Meilin set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.880
against the red moon and as she soars through&nbsp;
the air you can also spot a billboard for Air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.520
Canada with a particularly fitting tagline&nbsp;
for Mei in this moment, "Fly With Us".
&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.000
A running joke and theme in Turning Red&nbsp;
centres around the number four which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.280
in many East Asian cultures including China&nbsp;
is frequently viewed as an unlucky number.
&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.440
Four is the worst number.
You know,
&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.640
Vivian was due on the fourth, but&nbsp;
I held her in until the fifth...
&nbsp;

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:22.080
It might also be an extra, subconscious reason&nbsp;
for Ming's dislike of the boy band 4*Town.
&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.480
Why are they called "4-Town"&nbsp;
if there are five of them?
&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:29.280
The gag continues at the end of the movie when&nbsp;
we discover Meilin's grandma had to put her panda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.040
spirit in a 4*Town medallion. 
So unlucky.
&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:38.240
And notice how her losing hand consists of an&nbsp;
ace and four cards with the number four.
&nbsp;

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.480
Red Panda Mei's reaction to&nbsp;
spotting Devon at the Daisy Mart,
&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.880
Awooga!
(GASPS)
&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.320
is a hat-tip to the "Awooga!" meme which drew&nbsp;
on Tex Avery's cartoon Red Hot Riding Hood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.600
which features a thirsty wolf&nbsp;
watching a nightclub singer.
&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:58.400
And intriguingly, opposite the convenience&nbsp;
store, there's a Café Mémé, a play on Ming's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.120
nickname for her daughter Mei-Mei, but&nbsp;
also a visual pun on the word meme.
&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.800
Turning Red is set in 2002 and there's&nbsp;
some fun noughties references in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.520
movie such as the "Jokia" phone that Mei&nbsp;
uses, a reference to how Nokia phones&nbsp;&nbsp;

